et Lander 001 is uniformly scaled to 75% at its root. Internal proportions, state machine, thruster behavior, and scene anchor are unchanged.\nRoom 001's canonical eye is raised modestly from y=1.62 to y=1.78 and its initial pitch is biased slightly downward (-0.095 rad). Existing one-finger look and pinch-dolly semantics are unchanged; no new gesture is introduced.\nThis is a composition correction discovered by the flight coupon: preserve the human-scale controller and make the tabletop readable before adding cargo detail.\n\nV1.38 TABLETOP TOY \u2014 PALLET LANDER FLIGHT SUBSTRATE\nRoom 001 now owns its first stateful content module: Pallet Lander 001. The current geometry is intentionally a crude flight coupon, not the finished cargo lander.\nSemantic hierarchy:\nRoom 001 -\u003e Contents -\u003e Pallet Lander 001 -\u003e Chassis / Legs / Main Thruster / Station-Keeping Thrusters / Controls.\nThe entire vehicle is parented beneath one PalletLander001 root. Flight motion is applied only to that root so future cargo, air blister, telescoping stage, drones, and other descendants inherit flight without modifying hover code.\nThe lander's current scene anchor is the middle of the workbench. This is a convenient scene-local anchor, not lore or resolved station geography.\nA physical button on the chassis owns the interaction. There is no new global flight UI.\nCurrent state loop:\nLANDED -\u003e RETRACTING_GEAR -\u003e LIFTING -\u003e HOVERING -\u003e LANDING -\u003e DEPLOYING_GEAR -\u003e LANDED.\nThe main center thruster carries lift. Four canted corner thrusters are station-keeping/attitude devices and pulse during hover corrections.\nInterior tap interaction is distinct from interior look: a short stationary tap may activate an in-scene interactive; dragging still changes gaze and pinch still dollies.\nSwitching between EXTERIOR and INTERIOR does not reset the lander. Its local state persists because scene visibility is not world-state mutation.\nThis pass intentionally does NOT add cargo, blister, telescoping cargo stage, drones, destinations, or raster output. The purpose is to prove the parent transform and local state-machine boundary while the vehicle is still a flying brick.\n\nV1.37 PROXY TOPOLOGY \u2014 DRUM B IS ATTACHED TO THE ARM END\nHabitat Drum B is no longer independently positioned in the Room 001 exterior composition. Its proxy position is derived from Habitat Arm West's far endpoint after the arm's +90\u00b0 Y orientation transform.\nFor this proxy the arm is 5.6 units long, so the far endpoint is 2.8 local units along its rotated long axis. Drum B is placed directly beyond that endpoint with only a small collar clearance. The minimal connective frame is derived at the same attachment location.\nThis preserves the unresolved metric status of Room 001 while restoring the already-known topological fact: Habitat Arm West terminates at Habitat Drum B.\n\nV1.36 VIEW COMPOSITION \u2014 ARM IS PERPENDICULAR TO THE WINDOW PLANE\nThe Habitat Arm West proxy is authored along local X. V1.35 left that local axis unrotated, which made the arm run laterally across the window plane. The complete proxy module is now rotated +90\u00b0 about Y so its long axis projects into/out of the window plane.\nThis is an observer-composition transform on the situated proxy only; it does not alter authoritative station topology or resolve Room 001's metric location.\n\nV1.35 STARTUP REPAIR \u2014 PROXY USES THE ACTUAL HULL TEXTURE FACTORY\nV1.34 referenced a nonexistent `makeSurfaceTexture()` helper. Because the proxy is constructed during startup, that ReferenceError aborted the optional frame/proxy stage and triggered the visible FRAME LAYER ERROR fallback.\nThe situated exterior proxy now uses the already-established `makeHullTexture(seed)` factory\u2014the same procedural hull skin vocabulary used by station modules. No new texture factory or startup dependency is introduced.\nThis is a runtime-name failure that static syntax checking cannot detect; optional visual additions must call existing substrate APIs by their actual names.\n\nV1.34 WINDOW COMPOSITION CORRECTION \u2014 RIGHT-ANCHORED, SIDE-ON ARCHITECTURE\nThe Room 001 exterior proxy is recomposed rather than metrically solved. Habitat Arm West now runs perpendicular to the window/view axis and occupies a substantial lower-right span. Habitat Drum B is placed beyond it at the right corner, side-on and intended to remain fully legible rather than peeking above the sill.\nThe proxy no longer attempts to discover texture maps through module userData. It owns procedural CanvasTextures generated by the same surface-texture factory and semantic seeds, so its pressure skins visibly participate in TEXTURE ON/OFF.\nThis remains a situated view representation, not a declaration of Room 001's exact coordinates.\n\nV1.33 SITUATED EXTERIOR PROXY \u2014 VISIBILITY WITHOUT METRIC COMMITMENT\nRoom 001's window now looks from a conceptual location somewhere along habitat-arm-forward across a composed proxy of habitat-arm-west and habitat-drum-b.\nThese are not authoritative duplicate world objects and do not establish Room 001 coordinates. They are a situated visual representation of already-known station architecture while the room's exact metric placement remains unresolved.\nThe proxy reuses the station's pressure-skin, orderly Detail, and orange Frame vocabulary. Its architecture is placed somewhat below the aperture sightline so textured and detailed exterior surfaces remain legible from human scale.\nDETAIL and TEXTURE controls propagate to the proxy. No planet, invented deck plan, new windows, or decorative exterior lore is introduced.\n\nV1.32 INTERIOR SKIN DELTA \u2014 SLIGHTLY STRONGER READ + SEMANTIC FASTENERS\nInterior procedural contrast is raised modestly rather than redesigned: panel seams, floor joints, work-surface lines, and wear haze read slightly more clearly at human scale.\nSmall rivet/fastener marks are introduced only along plausible shell panel seams and floor plate joints. They are texture information, not free-floating decoration, and do not appear on glass, frame, lights, door, or workbench legs.\nThe workbench skin remains comparatively restrained so the architecture establishes scale without every surface competing for attention.\n\nV1.31 STARTUP SAFETY \u2014 LATE SYSTEMS MAY NOT ENTER EARLY LEXICAL TDZ\nV1.30 broke startup because the exterior texture initializer called setAllTextures(true) before the later interior `const IM` binding had initialized. `typeof` does not protect access to a lexical binding inside its temporal dead zone.\nInterior material handles are now published only after construction through a startup-safe runtime registry. Early exterior initialization never touches the late interior binding; later user toggles synchronize both systems.\nThe base station and its debug fallback remain authoritative: an optional interior skin system must never prevent the substrate from booting.\n\nV1.30 INVARIANT \u2014 HUMAN-SCALE TEXTURE REMAINS SKIN\nRoom 001 gains three restrained procedural CanvasTexture families: manufactured wall/ceiling panels, broad industrial floor plates, and a finer utilitarian work-surface skin.\nThey alter only surface appearance. Room geometry, pressure openings, frame, door, workbench structure, lights, and glass remain independent.\nFrame, door, workbench legs, lights, and glass intentionally stay materially clean so procedural texture does not become procedural greeble.\nThe existing global TEXTURE control now governs these interior skins as well as exterior module skins.\n\nV1.29 INVARIANT \u2014 FRAME THICKNESS IS A TRUE EDGE OFFSET\nThe continuous viewport ring's outer boundary is computed by offsetting every aperture edge by a constant perpendicular distance and intersecting adjacent offset lines.\nThis preserves visually consistent frame thickness through both orthogonal and chamfered edges instead of making diagonal corners artificially fat