ow assembly itself; it is not cosmetic trim laid over rectangular glass.\n\nV1.25 INVARIANT \u2014 PINCH DIRECTLY ADDRESSES THE DOLLY TRACK\nInterior entry and reset begin at the midpoint of the permitted fore/aft camera track rather than at either extreme.\nA single two-finger pinch gesture is intentionally high-gain: from any current dolly position, one ordinary continuous pinch has sufficient range to reach either track endpoint.\nThe gesture is anchored to the dolly position at pinch start, so movement is continuous and does not accumulate across repeated pinch gestures.\nThe existing hard forward boundary and rear-wall safety boundary remain authoritative.\n\nV1.24 INVARIANT \u2014 CAMERA TRACK RESPECTS THE ROOM ENVELOPE\nRoom 001's rear half is extended without moving the window/front wall or canonical eye point.\nThe rear wall and its doorway move together because the doorway belongs to that boundary.\nThe permitted backward dolly grows by less than the room extension and retains a deliberate safety margin before the rear wall.\nA camera track must never terminate inside enclosing geometry; room-envelope changes and camera bounds are related semantic constraints.\n\nV1.23 INVARIANT \u2014 MOBILE INTERIOR CAMERA = LOOK + BOUNDED DOLLY\nThe interior mobile interaction vocabulary is intentionally small:\none-finger drag rotates gaze from a situated human-scale viewpoint;\ntwo-finger pinch controls a fore/aft camera dolly along the room's canonical axis.\nThe canonical eye point is the hard forward boundary. Dolly may retreat from it and may return to it, but may never advance farther into the room.\nThis preserves the successful fixed-camera feel while allowing the observer to back up for composition and spatial inspection.\nIf more retreat is needed, enlarge the room/camera allowance rather than weakening the forward boundary.\n\nV1.22 INVARIANT \u2014 INTERIOR OBSERVATION IS SITUATED LOOK\nExterior inspection and interior observation intentionally use different camera semantics.\nExterior one-finger drag orbits the observer around the station.\nInterior one-finger drag changes yaw/pitch of a fixed human-scale eye point; it never changes eye position, orbit radius, Room 001 transform, or StationRoot.\nPinch is not optical zoom in the interior: it is a bounded camera dolly. Wheel remains disabled for the interior probe.\nReset in interior mode restores the canonical eye position and forward gaze.\nA tap in interior mode does not perform exterior module selection.\n\nV1.22 LESSON \u2014 CAMERA BEHAVIOR IS PART OF WORLD SEMANTICS\nThe V1.21 escape bug showed that identical gestures cannot safely share identical spatial meaning across observational scales.\nInteraction controllers therefore belong to view semantics, not merely input plumbing.\nThe accidental free-camera view was useful development evidence but is not the normal interior experience.\n\nV1.21 INVARIANT \u2014 CONTAINMENT DOES NOT IMPLY COORDINATES\nRoom 001 is contained by Habitat Drum A but has no resolved metric placement, deck, azimuth, or exterior transform.\nIts local frame belongs to the station, while its exact location within the containing module remains unresolved until an experience requires resolution.\nThe exterior window establishes only that Room 001 has an exterior-facing view; exact window placement is unresolved.\nThe window may therefore use a station-world view representation without asserting a fabricated deck plan.\n\nV1.21 INVARIANT \u2014 OBSERVER TRANSFORMS ARE NOT WORLD TRANSFORMS\nExterior orbit is an inspection operation by the observer. It must not be interpreted as an in-world physical rotation event.\nInterior entry changes observational scale/view mode, not world state.\nRoom 001 remains station-local regardless of the exterior observer's last inspection angle.\n\nV1.21 INTERIOR PROBE\nThe first interior deliberately establishes only structural human-scale anchors: shell, floor, ceiling, one doorway, one work surface, one large exterior window, and functional light.\nNo interior texture, decorative clutter, interior greebling, complete deck plan, locomotion system, or avatar is implied.\nThe workbench is reserved as a future tangible-material staging surface.\n\nV1.20 INVARIANT \u2014 ORIENTATION IS A SEMANTIC NORMAL\nDirectional modules are authored once in a canonical local frame (+Z for EndCap) and the complete module is quaternion-aligned to an explicit outward normal.\nChild geometry must not independently encode world-axis cases or signs.\nThe outward normal is semantic module state and is stored on the EndCap.\nThis guarantees that pressure bulkhead, taper, Detail, and future hatch/window geometry all agree about \"outward.\"\nAxis-plus-sign special casing is obsolete for EndCap orientation.\n\nV1.19 LESSON \u2014 TERMINATIONS COME FROM TOPOLOGY, WITH ORIENTATION\nEnd caps must only occupy genuinely exposed pressure ports.\nA port already consumed by another module (the west arm into Drum B) must not also receive a cap.\nEvery termination must carry an explicit outward direction; V1.20 represents that direction as a semantic normal rather than axis-plus-sign special cases.\nThe central trunk has two exposed axial boundaries, so its top and bottom each receive correctly outward-facing caps.\nThe forward cap was already correct and is preserved.\nThis pass exposes the next architectural target: derive termination placement from port occupancy rather than maintaining a hand-authored cap list.\n\nV1.18 INVARIANT \u2014 END CAP IS A PRESSURE-TOPOLOGY MODULE\nAn EndCap is a semantic one-port PressureModule, not a loose decorative disk.\nIts inward port terminates an exposed pressure boundary with a heavy collar, recessed bulkhead, and shallow exterior cap.\nEnd caps inherit the same module-owned SurfaceMaterial and Detail systems as other architecture.\nThe current caps seal the forward and east-upper expansion ends plus the central trunk top and bottom. The west end is not capped because Drum B already consumes that connection.\nFuture one-port termination modules (window, airlock, docking port, equipment cap) can reuse the same connection grammar.\n\nV1.17 INVARIANT \u2014 FULL PRESSURE-SKIN TEXTURE + RESPONSIVE CONTROLS\nDETAIL and TEXTURE live in a wrapping control dock and cannot overlap on narrow displays.\n\nV1.16 INVARIANT \u2014 MODULE-OWNED SURFACE TEXTURE\nEach semantic module owns its PressureVolume surface-material state.\nProcedural CanvasTextures provide restrained plate variation, seams, service bands, and sparse wear.\nTexture covers the full exterior PressureVolume architecture of every module, including hulls, collars, belts and bulkheads; inward cavity shells remain clean. Structure and Detail remain independent.\nTEXTURE ON/OFF is an aggregate control over module-owned material states.\nPer-module texture control is exposed through stationToy.setTexture(id,on).\n\nV1.15 INVARIANT \u2014 DETAIL PROMOTED; EXPERIMENT SCAFFOLD REMOVED\nThe A/B greeble experiment is complete.\nLegacy hand-authored boxy Detail children are purged.\nThe winning generated style-guide field is now the canonical per-module Detail layer.\nDETAIL ON/OFF is the sole global visibility control.\nNo separate Greeble mode or Greeble UI remains.\n\nV1.14 STYLE GUIDE \u2014 UNIFORM MANUFACTURED SURFACE LANGUAGE\nThe generated reference image is now treated as the visual target.\nGreebles are organized, not sprinkled: regular panel stations, repeated rows/columns, embedded 3/4/5-bit arrays, and coherent three-pipe bundles.\nDrum panels and pipes follow curvature. Arm detail aligns to stable longitudinal surface lanes.\nDetail remains thin and subordinate to the hull.\nConnection points receive enlarged exclusion zones; generators reject detail near semantic ports and arm endpoints.\nThe uniform style-guide generator is canonical Detail; DETAIL ON/OFF is its sole global visibility control.\n\nV1.10 INVARIANT \u2014 NO ANONYMOUS JUNCTIONS\nThe legacy migration that promoted leftover pressure meshes into junction-\u003cuuid\u003e modules is removed.\nLoose collars/belts/bulkheads are not topology and are discarded instead of becoming selectable modules.\nA junction may exist only when explicitly authored as a named pressure module through the port/connection grammar.\n\nV1.9 INVARIANT \u2014 MODULES OWN PORTS; CONNECTIONS FOLLOW\nReusable PressureModule, DrumModule, PressurePort, and ArmConnection classes now hold connection rules once.\nDrum A has been moved farther east; Habitat Arm East derives its span from the trunk port to Drum A's west port.\nDrum B and Habitat Arm West now use the identical port-to-port connection mechanism.\nIndividual drums no longer encode special arm lengths.\n\nV1.8 INVARIANT \u2014 UNIVERSAL PRESSURE BRANCHES\nThere is no branch-riser module type. Risers, links, continuations, and branches are ordinary Habitat Arms.\nNew pressure arms are authored between explicit attachment surfaces via pressureArmBetween().\nA branch from a trunk begins at the trunk exterior surface; it does not originate at the trunk center and overlap outward.\nThe attachment relationship, not visual overlap, defines the connection.\n\nV1.7 INVARIANT \u2014 ENDPOINT-AUTHORED MODULE CONNECTIONS\nHabitat Arm East is no longer positioned by an independent center + nominal length.\nIts endpoints are derived from Central Trunk's exterior surface and Habitat Drum A's west attachment plane.\nThe arm therefore terminates at the drum surface instead of occupying the drum interior.\nIts derived frame and frame sockets consume the exact same connection length.\n\nV1.6 INVARIANT \u2014 DERIVED FRAME GRAMMAR\nOrange Structure.Frame geometry is derived from its owning PressureVolume, never authored as arbitrary free lines.\nArm frames use four exterior longitudinal rails plus end frames at radius + clearance.\nDrum frames use exterior rings and orthogonal tangent rails at radius + clearance.\nFrame sockets are generated from the same module-local profile.\nFuture cross-module continuity is socket-to-socket. Ordinary frame generation therefore cannot enter its own pressure volume.\nThe failed post-hoc collision/clipping approach is intentionally removed.\n\nV1.3 INVARIANT \u2014 LOCAL OWNERSHIP\nStationRoot is a transform/container only and owns no visible geometry.\nEvery visible object belongs to an addressable module.\nEach module owns PressureVolume, Structure.Primary, Structure.Frame, Detail, and AttachmentNodes.\nOrange frame geometry is module-local structural detail.\nAdjacent modules use matching frame-interface metadata so independent frames can visually continue across a joint.\nIf something spans modules and cannot belong cleanly to either, it must become an addressable junction/connector module.\n\nV1.1 DELTA \u2014 HOLLOW TOPOLOGY\n- The station's primary architecture is itself inhabitable.\n- Large hubs are hollow drum/chamber volumes, not pills.\n- Branching arms are hollow orthogonal pressure volumes, not rods or trusses.\n- Interiors are intentionally empty for now; hollow volume is the only interior requirement.\n- Every arm joins a hub/spine through visible collars and terminates at a physical bulkhead/attachment collar.\n- External trusses/braces/greebles are secondary support/detail layers, never substitutes for inhabited arms.\n- All primary architecture remains orthogonal for this pass. Radial capability is preserved but unused.\n- No floating decorative arc.\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript\u003e\nwindow.orbitalFail=(scope,e)=\u003e{clearTimeout(window.orbitalWatch);const f=document.getElementById('fault');f.style.display='block';f.textContent='ORBITAL CONSTRUCTION \u2014 '+scope+'\\n'+(e?.stack||e?.message||String(e));};\naddEventListener('error',e=\u003eorbitalFail('startup / runtime',e.error||e.message));addEventListener('unhandledrejection',e=\u003eorbitalFail('promise',e.reason));window.orbitalWatch=setTimeout(()=\u003e{if(!window.orbitalReady)orbitalFail('load watchdog','